---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const BASE_URL = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="AI Research Radar — Learnings">
  <main class="project">
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="crumb"><a href={BASE_URL + 'projects/research-radar'}>AI Research Radar</a></p>
      <h1>Learnings</h1>
      <nav class="actions">
        <span class="btn current">Learnings</span>
        <a class="btn" href={BASE_URL + 'projects/research-radar/design-decisions'}>Design decisions</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/alawal1/research-radar" target="_blank" rel="noopener">⌥ Code</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section>
      <p>
        The agent worked locally on the first try. It wrote to a Google Sheet, logged the
        run, and produced a clean digest. Then I deployed it to GitHub Actions and it
        broke — a <code>SpreadsheetNotFound: 404</code> that made no sense.
      </p>
      <p>
        I checked everything. The sheet ID was correct. The service account email was
        shared with the sheet as Editor. The base64-encoded credentials decoded to valid
        JSON with the same project ID and the same service account. The API was enabled.
        Locally, the exact same code with the exact same credentials worked. In GitHub
        Actions, it returned 404.
      </p>
      <p>
        I spent hours on this. I added debug steps to the workflow to print the length of
        each secret, the hex dump of the sheet ID, the decoded service account email. Every
        check came back identical to what I had locally. The 404 never went away.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I stopped debugging and changed the architecture instead. The cloud agent now
        writes sheet rows to a CSV. A separate local script reads the CSV and appends it to
        Google Sheets. The cloud never touches Sheets. The 404 is gone.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Lesson: when a bug appears in one environment and every visible variable
        matches, stop looking for the difference and change the architecture so the
        environment doesn't matter.</strong> "Route around it" is a legitimate engineering
        decision, not a failure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href={BASE_URL + 'projects/research-radar'}>← Back to project</a></p>
  </main>

  <style>
    .project { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; line-height: 1.65; }
    .hero { text-align: center; margin: 3rem 0 3rem; }
    .hero h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
    .crumb { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.6; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
    .crumb a { text-decoration: none; }
    .actions { display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .btn {
      display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;
      padding: 0.55rem 1.1rem; border-radius: 999px;
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
      border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
      text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 500;
      transition: background 0.15s ease;
    }
    .btn:hover { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18); }
    .btn.current { opacity: 0.45; cursor: default; pointer-events: none; }
    section { margin: 3rem 0; }
    code { background: rgba(255,255,255,0.08); padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; }
    .back { margin-top: 4rem; opacity: 0.7; }
  </style>
</Layout>
